# Atlas Trace Headers - Required on Every Class B+ Source File

Every Class B+ source file (any `.py`, `.ts`, or `.tsx` under `libs/` or
`services/`) MUST carry exactly one Atlas trace header that names the
file's owning Atlas Design Output (DO) and parent Design Input (DI).

This is the only place we encode design-control IDs in source. All
other inline references to legacy `*-LEO-AI-N` IDs were removed in
Phase 13c; do not reintroduce them.

CI enforces this gate via `scripts/atlas/check_atlas_trace_headers.py`.
A PR that adds a Class B+ file without a trace header (and does not
add it to the allow-list) will fail the build.

## Format - Python

The block lives at the very top of the module docstring, immediately
after the one-line summary. Use Atlas dcIds (UUID8 form), never
legacy `*-LEO-AI-N` ids.

```python
"""Cohort analytics helpers.

Atlas trace:
  DO: DO-A6D3FE39
  DI: DI-9F2C1B4E
  Risks: R-72C9B609
  Look up via: get_design_control(dc_type="design-output", dc_id="DO-A6D3FE39")

Rest of the module docstring continues here ...
"""
```

If the file has no module docstring, create one whose only content is
the Atlas trace block. Do not put code above the docstring.

## Format - TypeScript / TSX

The block lives in a JSDoc comment at the top of the file, before any
imports.

```typescript
/**
 * Cohort analytics helpers.
 *
 * Atlas trace:
 *   DO: DO-A6D3FE39
 *   DI: DI-9F2C1B4E
 *   Risks: R-72C9B609
 *   Look up via: get_design_control(dc_type="design-output", dc_id="DO-A6D3FE39")
 */
```

## Field rules

- `DO:` REQUIRED. Every Class B+ file has exactly one owning DO.
  Files that legitimately implement multiple DOs put the
  lexicographically smallest dcId on the `DO:` line and additional
  dcIds on an `Also implements:` line.
- `DI:` REQUIRED. The DI is `IMPLEMENTS`-linked to the DO. If a DO
  has multiple parent DIs, list them comma-separated on a single line.
- `Risks:` OPTIONAL. Only include risks that Atlas links to one of
  the DIs above (`MITIGATES` edges in `traceability.risks`).
- `Look up via:` REQUIRED. Documents the exact MCP call to read the
  authoritative spec. Always cite the `DO:` dcId here, not the DI.

## When you create a new Class B+ file

1. Identify or create the owning DI in Atlas (`create_design_input`
   on `PROD-LEO-CLINICAL-DECISION-SUPPORT-20260515-118182E4`).
2. Identify or create the owning DO (`create_design_output`) and link
   it to the DI (`link_design_controls`).
3. Set the DO's `source_reference` to the new file's path
   (`update_design_output(source_reference=...)`).
4. Add the Atlas trace header to the new file using the dcIds you
   just got back. Include any `MITIGATES`-linked risks.
5. The CI gate will pass.

## When you move or rename a Class B+ file

1. The header travels with the file (it lives inside the docstring).
2. Update the owning DO's `source_reference` in Atlas via
   `update_design_output(source_reference=<new path list>)`.
3. The CI gate will keep passing because the file still has the
   header.

## Excluded paths (no header required)

The CI gate skips:

- Test files: `tests/`, `e2e/`, `__tests__/`, `test_*.py`,
  `*.test.ts(x)`, `conftest.py`. Tests trace to a VT, not a DO; the
  VT id appears in the test docstring per `vt-enforcement.mdc`.
- `__init__.py` (no executable surface beyond re-exports).
- Declaration-only `.d.ts`.
- Build outputs: `node_modules/`, `.next/`, `dist/`, `build/`.

## Allow-lists (use sparingly)

Two CSVs allow files through the CI gate without a header:

- `compliance/atlas-migration-evidence/phase13c-uncovered.csv` -- 591
  files identified during Phase 13a as having no Atlas DI/DO
  ownership in the migration ledgers. These need to be triaged into
  Atlas DOs before they can be removed from the allow-list.
- `compliance/atlas-migration-evidence/phase13c-allow-list.csv` -- a
  hand-maintained list of files added after the Phase 13a snapshot
  that need owning-DO triage. Add a row when you must temporarily
  bypass the gate, with a `reason` and a `added_at` date. Treat each
  row as a follow-up TODO.

If a header is missing in CI, prefer adding the header (option 1)
over allow-listing.

## Forbidden after Phase 13c

- Any reference of the form `(DI|DO|UN|VT|VAL|R)-LEO-AI-\d+` outside
  `compliance/atlas-migration-evidence/**` and `scripts/atlas/**`.
  Phase 13c stripped them; do not reintroduce.
- Multiple `Atlas trace:` blocks in one file.
- A trace block that names a legacy id.
- A trace block where the `DO:` field cannot be resolved to a real
  Atlas dcId.

## Re-running the stamper

If you bulk-add files (e.g. a new service), you can re-run the
Phase 13c stamper to add headers in bulk:

```bash
python3 scripts/atlas/phase13c_build_atlas_cache.py
python3 scripts/atlas/phase13c_stamp_headers.py --apply
python3 scripts/atlas/check_atlas_trace_headers.py
```

The stamper is idempotent: re-running on already-stamped files makes
no changes.

## Why this matters

Atlas is the single source of truth (see `design-controls-first.mdc`).
Without an in-file header pointing at the owning DO, code drifts
silently from its design control. The header gives:

- forward trace: code -> DO (and via Atlas, DO -> DI -> UN -> Risks).
- reverse trace: Atlas DO `source_reference` -> file path.
- regulatory evidence: any audit can grep `Atlas trace:` and confirm
  every Class B+ file is governed.
